  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain returns Thursday, with a transition to upslope \nsnow showers for the mountains Friday. Colder temperatures into the \nweekend.\n\nDry weather will continue for a couple more days until rain \nreturns on Thursday, as a surface low pressure system brings a \ncold front through the area as it pivots towards the northeast. \nThe surface low is forecast to track roughly along the eastern \nseaboard northward into New England with some degree of \nwraparound moisture maintaining precipitation chances over the \nmountains in the favored upslope areas through the end of the \nwork week. With the associated 500mb trough lingering across the\neastern US through the end of the weekend, multiple shortwaves \ntraveling through the trough will bring reinforcing waves of \ncold air. As this cold air reaches the area by Friday, expect a \ntransition from rain to snow over the mountain areas. Persistent\nnorthwesterly flow will keep upslope snow showers in the West \nVirginia mountains for a few days. Warm ground surface \ntemperatures will limit initial snowfall accumulations as snow \nwill likely melt on contact at first, but once the ground cools \nenough as the event continues, could see some light \naccumulations. Once the surface low pulls far enough away into \nthe Atlantic, the moisture return will gradually end and snow \nflurries will diminish, by the Saturday afternoon to overnight \ntime frame. High pressure pushes into the region by Sunday, and \nthe remainder of the weekend will be dry, but cold.\n\nTemperatures will fall into the 30s and 40s for highs over the \nmountains Friday and Saturday, and low 50s in the Piedmont. Cloud \ncover over the western slopes will aid in keeping daytime \ntemperatures down, so may see those high temperatures trend a couple \ndegrees lower. Overnight lows will be in the 20s and 30s \nThursday night/Friday morning, then widespread 20s through the \nweekend. Gusty northwesterly winds will make these temperatures \nfeel even colder, especially after the above normal temperatures\nthat have been observed for the first half of the month. Be \nsure to dress appropriately for the cold over the weekend.",
